# Keep scope small

Deliver the **minimum sufficient** result, prove it once, then stop. Scope
control never reduces the requested outcome or quality.

## Lock the slice

Lock **Outcome**, **Mutation boundary**, **Minimum proof**, and **Stop**. Keep
this internal unless useful.

Supporting work is necessary only when omission makes the outcome incorrect or
incomplete, breaks a contract, or invalidates proof. It may cross
files or include tests. Reuse, generalization, convenience, speculative
hardening, alternatives, broad audits, and extra reviewers are separate
outcomes.

Only, read-only, audit-only, a named file, and a fixed count are hard boundaries.
Ask before crossing them. Relock only when the user changes the outcome.

## Execute directly

- Take the shortest reliable route. Do not add comparison, research, planning,
  or parallel review without concrete need.
- Use one writer for overlapping files and preserve unrelated work.
- Run targeted checks; after the last material change, run each relevant
  mandatory gate once. When output quality matters, inspect the real output;
  structural success alone is insufficient. Do not repeat unchanged gates or
  create reviewer loops.
- Fix a finding only when necessary for the outcome and inside the boundary;
  otherwise report and defer it.

## Complexity tripwire

Pause if support introduces a new subsystem, dependency, public API, framework,
state machine or lifecycle, or effort comparable to the primary task. State the
need and smallest next step. Ask unless it is the requested primary outcome.

## Stop

When outcome and proof pass, report and stop. Status questions open no new work.
Installation, commit, push, deployment, and later phases stay separate unless
requested.

